        },
        {
          "type": "toolRequest",
          "id": "toolu_vrtx_01YCfyY65aGMMRpxHzM1Lax7",
          "toolCall": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "name": "write",
              "arguments": {
                "path": "/tmp/nix-shell.pYvf7T/.tmpy3AVwm/tides.md",
                "content": "# Understanding Tides: The Moon's Influence on Our Oceans\n\n## Introduction\n\nTides are the regular rise and fall of ocean levels caused primarily by the gravitational forces exerted by the Moon and the Sun on Earth's waters. These rhythmic movements have shaped human civilization for millennia, influencing everything from fishing practices to maritime navigation. Understanding tides is essential for coastal communities and oceanographers alike.\n\n## The Gravitational Force Behind Tides\n\nThe Moon's gravitational pull creates a bulge on the side of Earth facing it, while another bulge forms on the opposite side due to the centrifugal effect. As Earth rotates, most coastal areas experience two high tides and two low tides per day. The Sun also contributes to tidal forces, though its effect is roughly half as strong as the Moon's due to its greater distance from Earth.\n\n## Types of Tides\n\n### Spring Tides\n\nSpring tides occur when the Sun and Moon align on the same side of Earth, combining their gravitational forces. This alignment happens during full and new moons, resulting in the most extreme tidal ranges—the highest highs and lowest lows of the month.\n\n### Neap Tides\n\nNeap tides occur when the Sun and Moon are positioned at right angles to each other relative to Earth. This configuration happens during quarter moons and results in the smallest tidal ranges, with less dramatic differences between high and low water levels.\n\n## Impact on Marine Life and Ecosystems\n\nTidal zones create unique ecosystems with specialized organisms adapted to harsh conditions. Creatures living in the intertidal zone must tolerate both submersion and exposure to air. Tidal currents transport nutrients and larvae across vast ocean distances, supporting the food web from plankton to whales.\n\n## Tidal Range by Location\n\nThe tidal range varies significantly depending on geography. The following table shows typical tidal ranges in some of the world's most notable locations:\n\n| Location | Country | Average Tidal Range (meters) | Type of Coast | Notable Feature |\n|----------|---------|------------------------------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Bay of Fundy | Canada | 15.0 | Funnel-shaped bay | World's highest tides |\n| Bristol Channel | United Kingdom | 12.9 | Estuary | Second highest in world |\n| Ungava Bay | Canada | 10.2 | Remote arctic bay | Extremely remote location |\n| Severn Estuary | United Kingdom | 10.1 | River estuary | Historic bore phenomenon |\n| Gulf of Mexico | USA | 0.5 | Open gulf | Minimal tidal variation |\n\n## Human Applications\n\nTidal energy represents a renewable power source with growing potential. Countries like South Korea, France, and Canada have developed tidal power plants that generate electricity from the predictable motion of tides. Additionally, fishing communities rely on tidal calendars to maximize their catches, and sailors must account for tidal currents when navigating coastal waters.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nTides represent one of Earth's most predictable and powerful natural phenomena. From the dramatic 15-meter swings in the Bay of Fundy to the minimal tides of the Gulf of Mexico, these oceanic movements profoundly shape our world. As we face climate change and seek sustainable energy solutions, tides continue to offer valuable insights and opportunities for humanity's future.\n"
              }
            }
          },
